From a8be7a8c66b8d0d6ccb468b4f42cfd9d081796c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MyNaai <dev@mynaai.in>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 16:21:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Shop catalog: filter categories in SQL, so the count matches
 the grid
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reported from the live shop, with a screenshot: the first load is fine, but
choosing a category shows two products while the button underneath still
reads "Show more products (88)".

The count was never wrong about its own query. `listCatalog` resolved
`category` as a SUPERSET — the requested id, the label an admin typed, and
every row whose `category` was empty or unrecognised — so a filtered request
still counted most of the catalog. The website then applied its own keyword
rules to those rows and rendered the two that really belong to the shelf,
while the load-more count kept quoting the superset. Search and gender had
the same shape, and no amount of client-side patching can reconcile a total
with a grid the total does not describe.

So the classification moves into the query:

- `src/services/shopCategoryRules.js` (new) is the taxonomy and its rules in
  one place — the same seven categories, the same ordered keyword table, the
  same precedence the website uses (an explicit value first, then the product
  name, then the broadest shelf). `categoryCaseSql()` compiles those rules to
  a `CASE` expression and `genderCaseSql()` does the same for gender.
- `listCatalog` compares that expression with `=`, so the `WHERE`, the
  `COUNT`, `totalProducts`, `totalPages` and the rendered page all describe
  one list. `findAndCountAll` needs no join: the expression only reads the
  row it is counting, so the count and the page cannot disagree.
- An explicit `category` is honoured even when this build has never seen it
  (a slug the admin added later stands for itself, exactly as the website
  treats it), and the two vocabularies for gender — men/women/unisex from the
  control, MALE/FEMALE/UNISEX in the column — resolve to the same value.
- Admin create/update no longer leaves a blank `category` when the admin
  meant to classify: they fill in what the name infers, the same fallback
  the shop uses.
- `npm run shop:backfill-categories` (dry run by default, `-- --apply` to
  write) fills the column for the rows that predate it, so the admin panel
  and any SQL report count the same shelves the shop shows. It only touches
  values that are not already a category id.
- `npm run shop:test-categories` pins the classification table; the website
  pins the same rows in `src/lib/shopCatalog.test.js`, so a rule that changes
  on one side fails on the other instead of quietly reproducing this bug.

Nothing else about the catalog changes: search, price range, stock and the
three sort orders behave as before, `pagination.applied` is still echoed, and
a request without a category is untouched.
